cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "deidbench: a reproducible PHI-detection benchmark for HL7 v2"
message: "If you use this benchmark, please cite it and quote the caveats alongside the numbers."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Ryan
    family-names: Dick
    email: ryan@activecrossing.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-3992-3303"
    affiliation: "Active Crossing LLC"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ryanadick/deidentified-ai-benchmark"
license: MIT
version: 0.2.1
identifiers:
  # Two kinds of DOI, and the distinction matters. Zenodo mints a concept DOI
  # for the record series and a separate version DOI for each release; the
  # concept DOI is the lower number. Citing a version DOI as though it were
  # the project pins every reader to one release forever.
  #
  # Cite the concept DOI unless you are quoting a specific figure. If you are,
  # cite the version you read — and check ERRATA.md, which records figures
  # corrected after release.
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.23623785"
    description: "Concept DOI for the project — resolves to the latest version"
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.23623786"
    description: "Version DOI for v0.1.0 — frozen to that release"
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.24224414"
    description: >-
      Version DOI for v0.2.0 — frozen to that release. Its spike-results
      summary gives the unseen Z-segment figure as 0.95, taken from the wrong
      detector arm; the correct value is 0.900. See ERRATA.md.
abstract: >-
  A reproducible benchmark for protected health information (PHI) detection in
  HL7 v2 messages. Existing de-identification tools and evaluations target
  free-text clinical notes, but clinical data moves as interoperability
  messages, where PHI sits in structured fields, non-standard segments,
  misused standard fields, and narrative text embedded in the message. This
  package generates synthetic HL7 v2 messages with planted, annotated PHI
  labelled by location class, and scores detectors with a per-location
  breakdown. All data is synthetic; no production data or PHI is involved.
keywords:
  - HL7
  - health information exchange
  - de-identification
  - protected health information
  - benchmark
  - health informatics
